# KNOWN GAP — the `Box[Trait]` form of the `equip Pt(Shifter):` mis-spelling
# BUILDS CLEAN and SEGVs. It is the worse half of `todo/t0025`, and the half no
# method-existence rejection reaches.
#
# `equip Pt(Shifter):` is a mis-spelling of `equip Pt with Shifter:` whose whole
# method block is silently discarded. Where the call goes through a plain
# receiver (`Pt p = Pt(1); p.shift()`) the checker now REJECTS it — pinned by
# `tests/fixtures/equip_paren_trait_spelling_rejected.gg`.
#
# ⛔ THROUGH A TRAIT OBJECT NOTHING CATCHES IT. `Box[Shifter] b = Box.new(Pt(1))`
# packs a vtable whose slot for the dropped method is never filled, so:
#
#     gg check  → "OK: no semantic errors"     (there IS a `shift` on `Shifter`)
#     gg build  → rc 0, NO DIAGNOSTIC AT ALL
#     ./binary  → rc 139 (SIGSEGV)
#
# Measured 2026-09-03 both before and after the `t1017`/`t1019` pair — the
# rejection cannot see this cell, because the method the user calls DOES exist
# on the type the checker resolved (the TRAIT). The trait object removed the
# linker, which was the only thing catching the other half.
#
# ⚠ THE SEVERITY BAND IN `t0025`'s HEADER IS TOO LOW FOR THIS PATH: a silent
# SEGV outranks the link error the item was originally filed for.
#
# INTENDED: `2` — either because `gg check` rejects the mis-spelled `equip` (and
# then this program never runs), or because the packed vtable refuses to leave a
# slot unfilled. What is not a legal outcome is a clean build that segfaults.
trait Shifter:
    int shift(self)

struct Pt:
    int x

equip Pt(Shifter):
    int shift(self):
        return self.x + 1

int main():
    Box[Shifter] b = Box.new(Pt(1))
    print(b.shift())
    return 0
